          "work": "cw07"
        }
      },
      "render": "lorenz"
    },
    {
      "id": "alternating-current",
      "name": "Energy as an oscillation between poles",
      "quote": {
        "text": "Archetypes play a key role in the synchronic dynamics of individuation by mobilizing psychic energy into a kind of alternating current between consciousness and the unconscious.",
        "cite": {
          "workTitle": "Archetypes & Strange Attractors",
          "year": "1997",
          "locator": "p. 28"
        }
      },
      "familyIds": [
        "sun",
        "fire",
        "serpent",
        "water",
        "sun-hero",
        "helios"
      ],
      "jung": {
        "text": "a current of energy necessarily presupposes two different states, or poles, without which there can be no current",
        "cite": {
          "workTitle": "Psychological Types",
          "year": "",
          "locator": "¶337 (pdf p194)",
          "work": "cw06"
        }
      },
      "render": "lorenz"
    },
    {
      "id": "cascade-into-chaos",
      "name": "Oscillation cascading into chaos",
      "quote": {
        "text": "The oscillatory nature of the tension of opposites leads to chaos when the oscillations themselves begin to oscillate",
        "cite": {
          "workTitle": "Archetypes & Strange Attractors",
          "year": "1997",
          "locator": "p. 112"
        }
      },
      "familyIds": [
        "dismemberment",
        "prima-materia",
        "nigredo",
        "nekyia",
        "night-sea-journey",
        "dragon"
      ],
      "jung": {
        "text": "what looked like death and destruction will settle down into a latent state of concord",
        "cite": {
          "workTitle": "Mysterium Coniunctionis",
          "year": "",
          "locator": "¶506 (pdf p272)",
          "work": "cw14"
        }
      },
      "render": "lorenz"
    },
    {
      "id": "symmetry-building",
      "name": "Symmetry-building bifurcation",
      "quote": {
        "text": "Thus, they make it possible for “either-or” situations to become more symmetrical",
        "cite": {
          "workTitle": "Archetypes & Strange Attractors",
          "year": "1997",
          "locator": "p. 91"
        }
      },
      "familyIds": [
        "coniunctio",
        "divine-pair",
        "yin-yang",
        "tao",
        "quaternity",
        "cross"
      ],
      "jung": {
        "text": "The confrontation of the two positions generates a tension charged with energy and creates a living, third thing",
        "cite": {
          "workTitle": "The Structure and Dynamics of the Psyche",
          "year": "",
          "locator": "¶189 (pdf p102)",
          "work": "cw08"
        }
      },
      "render": "julia"
    },
    {
      "id": "symbol-emergence",
      "name": "Symbols: chaos first, then symmetry",
      "quote": {
        "text": "Thus, symbols first generate chaos, then build symmetry.",
        "cite": {
          "workTitle": "Archetypes & Strange Attractors",
          "year": "1997",
          "locator": "p. 114"
        }
      },
      "familyIds": [
        "prima-materia",
        "vessel",
        "coniunctio",
        "lapis",
        "serpent",
        "fire"
      ],
      "jung": {
        "text": "The symbols act as transformers, their function being to convert libido from a “lower” into a “higher” form.",
        "cite": {
          "workTitle": "Symbols of Transformation",
          "year": "",
          "locator": "¶344 (pdf p215)",
          "work": "cw05"
        }
      },
      "render": "mandelbrot"
    },
    {
      "id": "phase-transition",
      "name": "Enantiodromia as phase transition",
      "quote": {
        "text": "For order is never pure. Like the yin and the yang, it carries within it the potential to turn into its opposite. The same is true for chaos.",
        "cite": {
          "workTitle": "Archetypes & Strange Attractors",
          "year": "1997",
          "locator": "p. 171"
        }
      },
      "familyIds": [
        "yin-yang",
        "tao",
        "trickster-figure",
        "mercurius",
        "dragon",
        "antichrist"
      ],
      "jung": {
        "text": "shadow contains within it the seed of an enantiodromia, of a conversion into its opposite.",
        "cite": {
          "workTitle": "The Archetypes and the Collective Unconscious",
          "year": "",
          "locator": "¶488 (pdf p283)",
          "work": "cw09i"
        }
      },
      "render": "lorenz"
    },
    {
      "id": "homoclinic-point",
      "name": "Homoclinic point",
      "quote": {
        "text": "a homoclinic point, which designates not only where a dynamic becomes unstable, but where it becomes stable as well.",
        "cite": {
          "workTitle": "Archetypes & Strange Attractors",
          "year": "1997",
          "locator": "p. 62"
        }
      },
      "familyIds": [
        "spiral",
        "labyrinth",
        "uroboros",
        "yin-yang"
      ],
      "jung": {
        "text": "every psychic development, whether individual or collective, possesses an optimum which, when exceeded, produces an enantiodromia, that is, turns into its opposite.",
        "cite": {
          "workTitle": "Alchemical Studies",
          "year": "",
          "locator": "¶294 (pdf p277)",
          "work": "cw13"
        }
      },
      "render": "julia"
    },
    {
      "id": "self-organization",
      "name": "Compensation as self-organization",
      "quote": {
        "text": "Because the shadow compensates consciousness, whenever we think we know what the shadow is, its opposite automatically begins to emerge from the unconscious.",
        "cite": {
          "workTitle": "Archetypes & Strange Attractors",
          "year": "1997",
          "locator": "p. 93"
        }
      },
      "familyIds": [
        "dragon",
        "nigredo",
        "sol-niger",
        "antichrist",
        "desert",
        "sulphur",
        "persona"
      ],
      "jung": {
        "text": "Since the psyche is a self-regulating system, just as the body is",
        "cite": {
          "workTitle": "The Structure and Dynamics of the Psyche",
          "year": "",
          "locator": "pdf p91",
          "work": "cw08"
        }
      },
      "render": "lorenz"
    },
    {
      "id": "stuck-attractor",
      "name": "A symptom as a stuck attractor",
      "quote": {
        "text": "It simply repeats itself over and over.",
        "cite": {
          "workTitle": "Archetypes & Strange Attractors",
          "year": "1997",
          "locator": "p. 129"
        }
      },
      "familyIds": [
        "mother-goddess",
        "dual-mother",
        "wolf",
        "monster",
        "dragon"
      ],
      "jung": {
        "text": "A conscious capacity for one-sidedness is a sign of the highest culture, but involuntary one-sidedness, i.e., the inability to be anything but one-sided, is a sign of barbarism.",
        "cite": {
          "workTitle": "Psychological Types",
          "year": "",
          "locator": "¶346 (pdf p197)",
          "work": "cw06"
        }
      },
      "render": "lorenz"
    },
    {
      "id": "chaotic-edge",
      "name": "Health at the edge of chaos",
      "quote": {
        "text": "chaos is not only unavoidable, but necessary.",
        "cite": {
          "workTitle": "Archetypes & Strange Attractors",
          "year": "1997",
          "locator": "p. 13"
        }
      },
      "familyIds": [
        "prima-materia",
        "nigredo",
        "night-sea-journey",
        "nekyia"
      ],
      "jung": {
        "text": "begins to have an accelerating and intensifying effect on the conscious process",
        "cite": {
          "workTitle": "The Structure and Dynamics of the Psyche",
          "year": "",
          "locator": "¶160 (pdf p91)",
          "work": "cw08"
        }
      },
      "render": "lorenz"
    },
    {
      "id": "fractal-irreducibility",
      "name": "Interpretation that never closes",
      "quote": {
        "text": "In effect, the constructive approach is asymptotic: it approaches certainty, but never achieves it.",
        "cite": {
          "workTitle": "Archetypes & Strange Attractors",
          "year": "1997",
          "locator": "p. 106"
        }
      },
      "familyIds": [
        "vessel",
        "god-image",
        "anthropos",
        "lapis",
        "tree"
      ],
      "jung": {
        "text": "a vessel which we can never empty, and never fill",
        "cite": {
          "workTitle": "The Archetypes and the Collective Unconscious",
          "year": "",
          "locator": "¶301 (pdf p190)",
          "work": "cw09i"
        }
      },
      "render": "mandelbrot"
    },
    {
      "id": "mandala-attractor",
      "name": "Mandala and fractal attractor",
      "quote": {
        "text": "Could mandalas and fractal attractors be two versions of the same reality?",
        "cite": {
          "workTitle": "Archetypes & Strange Attractors",
          "year": "1997",
          "locator": "p. 111"
        }
      },
      "familyIds": [
        "mandala",
        "quaternity",
        "circle",
        "cross",
        "lapis"
      ],
      "jung": {
        "text": "These structures not only express order, they also create it.",
        "cite": {
          "workTitle": "The Structure and Dynamics of the Psyche",
          "year": "",
          "locator": "¶870 (pdf p469)",
          "work": "cw08"
        }
      },
      "render": "mandelbrot"
    },
    {
      "id": "relationship",
      "name": "Relationship turns chaos orderly",
      "quote": {
        "text": "Chaos becomes orderly when it finds its reflection.",
        "cite": {
          "workTitle": "Archetypes & Strange Attractors",
          "year": "1997",
          "locator": "p. 155"
        }
      },
      "familyIds": [
        "divine-pair",
        "anima-figure",
        "soul-image",
        "maiden",
        "coniunctio"
      ],
      "render": "julia"
    },
    {
      "id": "manifold",
      "name": "The symbol as a manifold",
      "quote": {
        "text": "We go from stability to instability and hopefully—back again.",
        "cite": {
          "workTitle": "Archetypes & Strange Attractors",
          "year": "1997",
          "locator": "p. 69"
        }
      },
      "familyIds": [
        "sun",
        "serpent",
        "fire",
        "tree",
        "water"
      ],
      "jung": {
        "text": "The psychological mechanism that transforms energy is the symbol.",
        "cite": {
          "workTitle": "The Structure and Dynamics of the Psyche",
          "year": "",
          "locator": "¶88 (pdf p57)",
          "work": "cw08"
        }
      },
